# Go source is stored and checked out with LF, so gofmt (which normalizes to LF)
# and git agree on every platform — without this, git's autocrlf can leave CRLF
# in a Windows working tree and make `gofmt -l .` flag files that are actually
# clean in the repository.
*.go text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# The CI workflows embed shell scripts in `run:` blocks that execute on Linux
# runners; a CRLF checkout would feed \r into every command.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Fuzz corpus entries are parsed by `go test`; a CRLF checkout would rewrite
# seeds git stores with LF and risk them failing to load on Windows.
testdata/fuzz/** text eol=lf

# Standalone scripts (hack/) run under bash on both Linux CI and a Windows
# developer's git-bash. A CRLF checkout puts \r at the end of every line, which
# bash reads as part of the command — the failure is a baffling "$'\r': command
# not found" rather than anything pointing at line endings.
*.sh text eol=lf
